e area under the respective curve if the function is increasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is increasing over $x\\in [-1,1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039366/assets/d0e535f39c947e55b6dba151f723dd3390fe67c29a571b940294e90869acac21.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a65fd1f2-8e43-5248-ad7d-a2bb1f666ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302224} {:answer/id #uuid "1512b76d-6e60-5ca1-a9ca-28cb5503d393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302225} {:answer/id #uuid "93bfc5c2-c8ad-51a3-a48a-5fa66ab4385c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302226} {:answer/id #uuid "178e916e-a7d7-543a-9e0c-44733074d144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302227} {:answer/id #uuid "916f618b-4f27-523b-8c19-655323981f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178e916e-a7d7-543a-9e0c-44733074d144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302227} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e096b0e-5bcb-5eaf-aa9d-dda496241782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c38218b2-4da8-5951-b9a1-a2fdae26a28b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115737" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-6,3].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $6.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $k$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(0)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+(-2)+k)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(k+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3k+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $6$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+9 & =6 \\\\ 3k & =6-9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d16ccd57-77d7-5a10-a3aa-3cefb3978f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302231} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d8070e-fd81-56b2-85f4-c3bb03d65fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302232} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab14b54-b687-5384-8a33-dfa5004f9a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302233} {:answer/id #uuid "41a29ffe-7dfb-5bc5-be5b-967098f8a016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302234} {:answer/id #uuid "266b14c8-f0cc-5c6c-8ef6-ffc8679db6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d8070e-fd81-56b2-85f4-c3bb03d65fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302232} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1c4c87-7bb4-596b-8a24-9b1e6d4504af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0777d3ec-036e-5efa-8bf3-2c5eb2fd8c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-20314" :question/problem "A boat travels downstream on a river. The table below gives the speed $v(t)$, in miles per hour, of the boat at certain moments during its journey.\n\n| $t\\text{ (hours)}$ | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ | $1.3$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\text{ (mi/hour) }$ | $32$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ | $80$ | $65$ |\n\n Use the right Riemann sum for $v(t)$ over $[0,1.5]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total distance traveled by the boat.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=v(t)$ over $[0,1.5].$ \nIn this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the total distance traveled can be approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈v(0.3)Δ{t}_{1}+v(0.7)Δ{t}_{2}+v(1)Δ{t}_{3}+v(1.3)Δ{t}_{4}+v(1.5)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ | $1.3$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $-$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ | $80$ | $65$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the number of liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈35\\cdot 0.3+40\\cdot 0.4+65\\cdot 0.3+80\\cdot 0.3+65\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =10.5+16+19.5+24+13 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled by the boat. So, the distance is approximately $83\\,\\text{mi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a12b8fd-2cfd-5e38-9917-e9c17c62f461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a12b8fd-2cfd-5e38-9917-e9c17c62f461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302238} :answer-field/id #uuid "12c8e0d9-90f6-5b40-a59f-da2b4bbba80a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e4448e6-52c9-5987-8c18-bd04f202595b" :question/math-academy-id "q-346457" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[10,16].$\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $12$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area $A$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[10,16].$ \n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(12)Δ{x}_{1}+f(13.5)Δ{x}_{2}+f(16)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $12$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $2$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1.5+3\\cdot 2.5 \\\\  & =4+6+7.5 \\\\  & =17.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
